<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    24-IPC / examples/attacker-app / AndroidManifest.xml

    WHAT THIS IS
        The manifest of a deliberately boring third-party application whose only
        job is to answer one question honestly:

            "Can an ORDINARY app, with no special privileges, actually reach
             this component / socket / port?"

    WHY IT MATTERS
        `adb shell` runs as UID 2000 in the SELinux domain `shell`. That UID
        holds privileges no installed application has. A component you started
        with `am start` is NOT thereby proven reachable by an attacker. This APK
        runs as an ordinary `untrusted_app` UID in the 10000+ range, so what it
        can do, a malicious app can do. See Chapter 24 section 3.22 step 4 and
        ../../../77-UsefulCommands/labs/lab-03-exported-sweep.md.

    NOTE ON THE PROJECT LAYOUT
        The sources sit directly in this directory rather than in
        src/main/java/..., and build.gradle points the Android sourceSets here.
        That keeps the proof-of-concept to five readable files. If you prefer the
        conventional layout, move AndroidManifest.xml to src/main/ and the .java
        files to src/main/java/com/attacker/poc/ and delete the sourceSets block.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.attacker.poc">

    <!--
        THE ONLY PERMISSION WE DECLARE.

        INTERNET is protectionLevel="normal": it is granted automatically at
        install time, it is never shown to the user, and essentially every app on
        every device holds it. It is needed only for the loopback probe
        (127.0.0.1), because loopback is a *network* interface.

        Everything else this app does — starting activities, binding services,
        sending broadcasts, querying providers, connecting to abstract-namespace
        UNIX sockets — requires NO permission whatsoever. That is the point.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        =====================================================================
        THE <queries> ELEMENT — DO NOT DELETE THIS
        =====================================================================

        From targetSdk 30 (Android 11), Android applies *package visibility
        filtering*. An app can no longer see every other installed package by
        default. The practical consequences for a probe app are brutal and
        silent:

          * PackageManager.getPackageInfo(target, ...) throws
            NameNotFoundException, exactly as if the app were not installed.
          * resolveActivity() / queryIntentActivities() return null or an empty
            list for components that genuinely exist and are genuinely exported.
          * startActivity() with an explicit ComponentName fails with
            ActivityNotFoundException rather than SecurityException.

        None of those look like "filtered". They look like "not there". A tester
        who forgets this block concludes the target is unreachable and writes
        "no exported components are reachable from a third-party app" in a
        report. That is a false negative produced by their own manifest.

        Package visibility filtering is a privacy control, not a security
        boundary: a real attacker simply declares the target in <queries>, or
        holds QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES. It never makes an exported component
        unreachable. It only hides it from apps that did not ask.

        Edit the entries below for each engagement.
    -->
    <queries>
        <!-- 1. The target package, by name. The most reliable form. -->
        <package android:name="com.example.target" />

        <!-- Add every package in scope, including companion apps that share a
             signing key with the target. -->
        <!-- <package android:name="com.example.target.companion" /> -->

        <!-- 2. Providers must additionally be named by AUTHORITY. Declaring the
                package alone is NOT enough to resolve a content:// URI. -->
        <provider android:authorities="com.example.target.provider" />
        <provider android:authorities="com.example.target.files" />

        <!-- 3. Intent-shaped visibility: lets us see anything that handles a
                given action/scheme. Useful for deep-link squatting research,
                and for discovering which app claims a custom scheme. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
            <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
        </intent>

        <!--
            4. Last resort. QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES makes the whole device visible and
               removes the need for any of the above. It is a restricted
               permission on Google Play (an app needs a declared, approved use
               case), which is why real malware usually prefers the targeted
               forms above. On a test device it is harmless and convenient.

               Uncomment BOTH lines together, and say in your report which form
               you used, because "we needed QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" changes the
               attacker's cost.
        -->
        <!-- </queries>
        <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" />
        <queries> -->
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:label="Flashlight"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_compass"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light">

        <!--
            The probe itself. Exported and given a LAUNCHER filter purely so you
            can start it by tapping it, or with:
                adb shell am start -n com.attacker.poc/.ProbeActivity
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ProbeActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Flashlight">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            Chapter 24 section 9.3 launches this app as
            `com.attacker.poc/.MainActivity`. The alias makes that command work
            too, so either name starts the same probe.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:targetActivity=".ProbeActivity"
            android:exported="true" />

        <!--
            Where a STOLEN content-URI grant lands.

            In the confused-deputy exploit (Chapter 24 section 3.20) we ask the
            victim app to start an Intent that targets THIS activity and carries
            FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION on a content:// URI belonging to the
            victim's own non-exported provider. Because the victim app makes the
            startActivity() call, the framework records the VICTIM as the granter
            and issues a legitimate read grant to us.

            Exported so the victim app (a different UID) can start it.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".GrantSink"
            android:exported="true" />

        <!--
            Passive broadcast eavesdropper.

            Any app can register for any action that is not a <protected-broadcast>.
            If the target calls sendBroadcast() without a receiver permission and
            without setPackage(), the extras arrive here. This is the "sending
            direction" leak that manifest-only analysis of the *target* never
            shows, because the bug is in the target's sending code, not in any
            component it declares.

            priority is set to the maximum so that for ORDERED broadcasts we run
            first and can observe, rewrite the result, or abortBroadcast().
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".Eavesdropper"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter android:priority="2147483647">
                <action android:name="com.example.target.SESSION_READY" />
                <action android:name="com.example.target.LOCATION_UPDATE" />
                <action android:name="com.example.target.ACTION_VERIFY" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
            OPTIONAL: custom-scheme squatting.

            Uncomment and set the scheme to the target's own to test whether a
            sensitive flow (an OAuth redirect, a magic link, a password reset)
            can be intercepted. Chapter 24 section 3.19. Leave it commented off
            by default: a squatting filter changes device-wide link resolution
            and will interfere with your other tests.
        -->
        <!--
        <activity android:name=".SchemeSquatter" android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter android:priority="2147483647">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="targetapp" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        -->
    </application>
</manifest>
